{"config":{"lang":["en"],"separator":"[\\s\\-]+","pipeline":["stopWordFilter"],"fields":{"title":{"boost":1000.0},"text":{"boost":1.0},"tags":{"boost":1000000.0}}},"docs":[{"location":"","title":"Home","text":""},{"location":"#home","title":"Home\u00b6","text":""},{"location":"#welcome","title":"Welcome\u00b6","text":""},{"location":"#about-these-course-notes","title":"About these course notes\u00b6","text":""},{"location":"#authors","title":"Authors\u00b6","text":""},{"location":"#found-a-mistake","title":"Found a mistake?\u00b6","text":""},{"location":"chapters/0.%20Foundations/0.%20Introduction/","title":"An Introduction to AI","text":""},{"location":"chapters/0.%20Foundations/1.%20Definitions%20and%20Notation/","title":"Definitions and Notation","text":""},{"location":"chapters/0.%20Foundations/2.%20Probability%20Theory/0.%20Set%20Theory/","title":"Set Theory","text":""},{"location":"chapters/0.%20Foundations/2.%20Probability%20Theory/1.%20Random%20Experiments/","title":"Random Experiments","text":""},{"location":"chapters/0.%20Foundations/2.%20Probability%20Theory/2.%20Expectation%20and%20Variance/","title":"Expectation and Variance","text":""},{"location":"chapters/0.%20Foundations/2.%20Probability%20Theory/3.%20Bayes%20Theorem/","title":"Bayes' Theorem","text":""},{"location":"chapters/0.%20Foundations/3.%20Statistical%20Inference/0.%20Point%20Estimation/","title":"Point Estimation","text":""},{"location":"chapters/0.%20Foundations/3.%20Statistical%20Inference/1.%20Bias%20vs%20Variance/","title":"Bias vs Variance","text":""},{"location":"chapters/0.%20Foundations/3.%20Statistical%20Inference/2.%20Covariance/","title":"Covariance","text":""},{"location":"chapters/0.%20Foundations/3.%20Statistical%20Inference/3.%20Correlation%20vs%20Causation/","title":"Correlation vs Causation","text":""},{"location":"chapters/0.%20Foundations/3.%20Statistical%20Inference/4.%20CI%20and%20Uncertainty/","title":"Confidence Intervals and Uncertainty","text":""},{"location":"chapters/0.%20Foundations/4.%20Linear%20Algebra/0.%20Vectors%20and%20Matrices/","title":"Vectors and Matrices","text":"<p>$A v = \\lambda v $</p> <p>$A v = \\lambda v $</p>"},{"location":"chapters/0.%20Foundations/4.%20Linear%20Algebra/1.%20Matrix%20Operations/","title":"Matrix Operations","text":""},{"location":"chapters/0.%20Foundations/4.%20Linear%20Algebra/2.%20Linear%20Transformations/","title":"Linear Transformations","text":""},{"location":"chapters/0.%20Foundations/4.%20Linear%20Algebra/3.%20Eigenvalues%20and%20Eigenvectors/","title":"Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors","text":""},{"location":"chapters/0.%20Foundations/4.%20Linear%20Algebra/4.%20SVD/","title":"Singular Value Decomposition (SVD)","text":""},{"location":"chapters/0.%20Foundations/5.%20Calculus%20and%20Optimisation%20Basics/0.%20Gradients/","title":"Gradients","text":""},{"location":"chapters/0.%20Foundations/5.%20Calculus%20and%20Optimisation%20Basics/1.%20Chain%20Rule/","title":"The Chain Rule","text":""},{"location":"chapters/0.%20Foundations/5.%20Calculus%20and%20Optimisation%20Basics/2.%20Convexity/","title":"Convexity","text":""},{"location":"chapters/0.%20Foundations/5.%20Calculus%20and%20Optimisation%20Basics/3.%20Intro%20Gradient%20Descent/","title":"Introduction to Gradient Descent","text":""},{"location":"chapters/1.%20Data/0.%20introduction%20to%20data/","title":"Introduction to Data","text":""},{"location":"chapters/1.%20Data/0.%20introduction%20to%20data/#introduction-to-data","title":"Introduction to Data","text":"<p>Welcome to data. In this context, data refers to the collection of observations or measurements that we use to train, test, and validate our models. While algorithms often get the spotlight, data is arguably the most important part of everything we do in AI. The performance of any model is heavily influenced by both the quality and quantity of the data it learns from. This section of the notes is designed to help you build intuition when working with data. </p>"},{"location":"chapters/1.%20Data/0.%20introduction%20to%20data/#common-terminology","title":"Common Terminology","text":"<p>Before we go any further, let\u2019s quickly define a few terms that will appear constantly throughout these notes.</p> <p>Observations</p> <p>Observations are the individual data points, examples, or records used to train, test, or evaluate a model.</p> <p>As an example, consider the following table:</p> Student ID First Name Last Name GPA 12345678 Adam Johnson 4.8 23456781 Ben Stiller 6.4 34567812 Clair Miller 5.3 <p>where an observation from this table would be:</p> Student ID First Name Last Name GPA 12345678 Adam Johnson 4.8 <p>Features</p> <p>A feature is an individual measurable property or characteristic of the observations. As an example, the features of the table:</p> Student ID First Name Last Name GPA 12345678 Adam Johnson 4.8 23456781 Ben Stiller 6.4 34567812 Clair Miller 5.3 <p>would be \"Student ID\", \"First Name\", \"Last Name\" and \"GPA\".</p> <p>Labels</p> <p>The label, also known as the \"dependent variable\", \"response variable\", \"output variable\", or \"target\" is the specific outcome or metric that a model is trained to predict or classify based on a given set of features.</p> <p>Categorical and Numerical Data</p> <p>Features can be described as either numerical or categorical. </p> <p>Numerical data represents quantities that can be measured, such as height, temperature, or time.</p> <p>Categorical data represents groupings, such as brand, country, or gender.  </p> <p>Note</p> <p>It is common practice to label binary results as \"1\" or \"0\". These recordings represent categorical data and should not be treated as numerical data. </p> <p>Treating categorical data as numerical data will likely invalidate your results.</p>"},{"location":"chapters/1.%20Data/1.%20Exploratory%20Data%20Analysis/","title":"Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)","text":""},{"location":"chapters/1.%20Data/1.%20Exploratory%20Data%20Analysis/#exploratory-data-analysis-eda","title":"Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)","text":"<p>Welcome to Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA), the stage where we slow down and get to know our data before building any models. EDA is all about asking simple questions and assessing the quality of our data. By exploring how the data is distributed and how variables relate to one another, we are able to build an intuition that helps guide modelling decision. Getting this right will save you time, prevent subtle mistakes, and often lead to much better models down the track.</p>"},{"location":"chapters/1.%20Data/1.%20Exploratory%20Data%20Analysis/#what-is-exploratory-data-analysis","title":"What is Exploratory Data Analysis?","text":"<p>EDA is the process of getting to know a dataset before building any models. It involves using simple statistics and visualisations to understand how the data is structured, how variables relate to one another, and where potential issues might lie. The goal isn\u2019t to model yet, but to build intuition, so that later decisions are informed rather than assumptions.</p>"},{"location":"chapters/1.%20Data/0.%20Data%20Types%20and%20Structures/0.%20Tabular%20Data/","title":"Tabular Data","text":""},{"location":"chapters/1.%20Data/0.%20Data%20Types%20and%20Structures/1.%20Time%20Series%20Data/","title":"Time Series Data","text":""},{"location":"chapters/1.%20Data/0.%20Data%20Types%20and%20Structures/2.%20Image%20Data/","title":"Image Data","text":""},{"location":"chapters/1.%20Data/2.%20Preprocessing/0.%20Data%20Cleaning/","title":"Data Cleaning","text":""},{"location":"chapters/1.%20Data/2.%20Preprocessing/1.%20Encoding%20Categorical%20Variables/","title":"Encoding Categorical Variables","text":""},{"location":"chapters/1.%20Data/2.%20Preprocessing/2.%20Feature%20Engineering/","title":"Feature Engineering","text":""},{"location":"chapters/1.%20Data/2.%20Preprocessing/3.%20Data%20Normalisation/","title":"Normalisation and Standardisation","text":""},{"location":"chapters/1.%20Data/2.%20Preprocessing/4.%20Dimensionality%20Reduction/","title":"Dimensionality Reduction","text":""},{"location":"chapters/10.%20Kernel%20Methods/0.%20Kernel%20Functions/","title":"The Kernel Trick","text":""},{"location":"chapters/10.%20Kernel%20Methods/1.%20SVMs%20with%20Kernels/","title":"Support Vector Machines (SVMs) with Kernels","text":""},{"location":"chapters/10.%20Kernel%20Methods/2.%20Kernel%20Ridge%20Regression/","title":"Kernel Ridge Regression","text":""},{"location":"chapters/10.%20Kernel%20Methods/3.%20Kernel%20PCA/","title":"Kernel PCA","text":""},{"location":"chapters/11.%20Gaussian%20Processes/0.%20Introduction%20to%20Gaussian%20Processes/","title":"Introduction to Gaussian Processes","text":""},{"location":"chapters/11.%20Gaussian%20Processes/1.%20Nonparametric%20Bayesian%20Modelling/","title":"Nonparametric Bayesian Modelling","text":""},{"location":"chapters/11.%20Gaussian%20Processes/2.%20Covariance%20Functions%20and%20Kernels/","title":"Covariance Functions and Kernels","text":""},{"location":"chapters/11.%20Gaussian%20Processes/3.%20Gaussian%20Process%20Regression/","title":"Gaussian Process Regression","text":""},{"location":"chapters/11.%20Gaussian%20Processes/4.%20Gaussian%20Process%20Classification/","title":"Gaussian Process Classification","text":""},{"location":"chapters/12.%20Generative%20Models/0.%20Generative%20vs%20Discriminative%20Models/","title":"Generative vs Discriminative Models","text":""},{"location":"chapters/12.%20Generative%20Models/1.%20Latent%20Variable%20Models/","title":"Latent Variable Models","text":""},{"location":"chapters/12.%20Generative%20Models/2.%20VAEs/","title":"Variational Autoencoders (VAEs)","text":""},{"location":"chapters/12.%20Generative%20Models/3.%20GANs/","title":"Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs)","text":""},{"location":"chapters/12.%20Generative%20Models/4.%20Normalising%20Flows/","title":"Normalising Flows","text":""},{"location":"chapters/13.%20Performance/0.%20Model%20Evaluation%20Robustness%20and%20Stability/","title":"Model Evaluation: Robustness & Stability","text":""},{"location":"chapters/13.%20Performance/1.%20Calibration/","title":"Calibration","text":""},{"location":"chapters/13.%20Performance/2.%20Fairness%20Bias%20and%20Ethics/","title":"Fairness, Bias, and Ethics","text":""},{"location":"chapters/13.%20Performance/3.%20Computational%20Efficiency/","title":"Computational Efficiency","text":""},{"location":"chapters/2.%20Statistical%20Learning%20Theory/0.%20Learning%20as%20Function%20Approximation/","title":"Learning as Function Approximation","text":""},{"location":"chapters/2.%20Statistical%20Learning%20Theory/1.%20Loss%20Functions%20and%20Risk%20Minimisation/","title":"Loss Functions and Risk Minimisation","text":""},{"location":"chapters/2.%20Statistical%20Learning%20Theory/2.%20Empirical%20Risk%20Minimisation/","title":"Empirical Risk Minimisation","text":""},{"location":"chapters/2.%20Statistical%20Learning%20Theory/3.%20OverUnder%20fitting/","title":"Generalisation, Overfitting, and Underfitting","text":""},{"location":"chapters/2.%20Statistical%20Learning%20Theory/4.%20Regularisation/","title":"Regularisation","text":""},{"location":"chapters/2.%20Statistical%20Learning%20Theory/5.%20Bias-Variance%20Tradeoff/","title":"Bias-Variance Tradeoff","text":""},{"location":"chapters/2.%20Statistical%20Learning%20Theory/6.%20No%20Free%20Lunch%20Theorem/","title":"No Free Lunch Theorem","text":""},{"location":"chapters/2.%20Statistical%20Learning%20Theory/7.%20Generalisation%20Gap/","title":"Generalisation Gap","text":""},{"location":"chapters/2.%20Statistical%20Learning%20Theory/8.%20VC%20Dimension/","title":"VC Dimension","text":""},{"location":"chapters/2.%20Statistical%20Learning%20Theory/9.%20PAC%20Learning/","title":"PAC Learning","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/0.%20Supervised%20Learning%20Framework/","title":"Supervised Learning Framework","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/0.%20Supervised%20Learning%20Framework/#what-is-supervised-learning","title":"What is Supervised Learning?","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/0.%20Supervised%20Learning%20Framework/#regression-vs-classification","title":"Regression vs Classification","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/0.%20Supervised%20Learning%20Framework/#parametric-vs-non-parametric-models","title":"Parametric vs non-parametric models","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/0.%20Supervised%20Learning%20Framework/#code-example","title":"Code Example","text":"<p>This is a word with a definition on hover.</p>"},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/1.%20Classification%20Models/0.%20KNN%20%28C%29/","title":"K-Nearest Neighbors (Classification)","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/1.%20Classification%20Models/0.%20KNN%20%28C%29/#k-nearest-neighbors-classification","title":"K-Nearest Neighbors (Classification)","text":"<p>Welcome to \\(k\\)-Nearest Neighbours, or KNN for short, one of the most approachable classification algorithms in machine learning. At its core, KNN shows how we can make predictions simply by looking at similar examples in our data, relying on the idea that things which look alike often belong to the same class.</p>"},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/1.%20Classification%20Models/0.%20KNN%20%28C%29/#intuition","title":"Intuition","text":"<p>\\(k\\)-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) is a supervised learning algorithm used for both classification and regression tasks. KNN works by identifying the \\(k\\) closest neighbors (data points) to a given input and making a prediction based on the majority class (for classification) or the average value (for regression). Here, we\u2019ll focus on classification and begin with a simple real-world example to build some intuition.</p> <p>Suppose a new movie has just been released and you are trying to decide whether you should watch it. You have not seen the movie yourself; however, many of your friends have seen it and share similar movie tastes to yours.</p> <p>You ask a few friends for their opinions, and for each friend you also know how similar their tastes are to yours. To make your decision, you decide to only ask friends whose preferences are most similar to yours. Let\u2019s say you consider the opinions of your five most similar friends. If the majority of those friends enjoyed the movie, you would decide the movie is worth watching.</p> <p>This is the idea behind \\(k\\)-Nearest Neighbors: - Each friend represents a data point. - How similar a friend\u2019s taste is to yours corresponds to distance. - The value of \\(k\\) is the number of friends\u2019 opinions you choose to consider. - The final decision is made by taking a majority vote among those friends.</p> <p>It should be noted that if you had chosen a smaller number of friends (for example, \\(k=3\\)), your decision would depend on very few opinions and could change easily. Alternatively, if you had chosen a larger number of friends, you would include people whose tastes are less similar to yours, which might influence the decision in a different way.</p> <p>Additionally, it is generally encouraged to avoid choosing an even value for \\(k\\). When \\(k\\) is even, it is possible for the nearest neighbors to be split evenly between classes, resulting in a tie. In this case, the algorithm has no clear majority to determine the predicted class. Ties are typically resolved by selecting the class of the closest neighbor or by using distance-weighted voting.</p> <p>\\(k\\)-Nearest Neighbors applies this same reasoning to data. Instead of friends and movie preferences, we work with data points and features. Instead of opinions, we work with class labels. To classify a new data point, the algorithm looks at the \\(k\\) most similar points in the dataset and lets them decide the outcome.</p>"},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/1.%20Classification%20Models/0.%20KNN%20%28C%29/#formal-definition","title":"Formal Definition","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/1.%20Classification%20Models/0.%20KNN%20%28C%29/#implementing-a-knn-classifier-with-scikit-learn","title":"Implementing a KNN Classifier with Scikit-Learn","text":"Imports<pre><code>import numpy as np\n\nfrom sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.datasets import load_iris\nfrom sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score\n</code></pre> Load Iris dataset<pre><code>iris = load_iris()\nX = iris.data\ny = iris.target\n</code></pre> Split the data into training and testing sets<pre><code>X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.2, random_state=4702)\n</code></pre> KNN classifier<pre><code>knn = KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=3)\nknn.fit(X_train, y_train)\ny_pred = knn.predict(X_test)\n</code></pre> Evaluate the model<pre><code>accuracy = accuracy_score(y_test, y_pred)\nprint(f\"Accuracy: {accuracy:.4f}\")\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/1.%20Classification%20Models/0.%20KNN%20%28C%29/#implementing-a-knn-classifier-from-first-principles","title":"Implementing a KNN Classifier from First Principles","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/1.%20Classification%20Models/0.%20KNN%20%28C%29/#theory-problems","title":"Theory Problems","text":"<p>Theory Question 1</p> <p>In \\(K\\)-Nearest Neighbours, what does \\(K\\) represent?</p> Solution <p>\\(K\\) represents the chosen number of neighbours</p> <p>Question 2</p> <p>Determine what the red cross (test point) would be labelled as for \\(k = 3\\), \\(k = 5\\) and \\(k = 10\\).</p> <p></p> Solution \\(k = 3\\) <p>Where \\(k = 3\\), the \\(3\\) nearest neighbors to our test point are those circled in red. Therefore, by majority vote our prediction would be an orange dot.  </p> <p></p> Solution \\(k = 5\\) <p>Where \\(k = 5\\), we can see that we have 3 blue triangles and 2 orange dots, resulting in the prediction for our test point being a blue triangle. </p> <p></p> <p>Note</p> <p>There is no single \"rule\" that guarantees the best choice of \\(k\\) for all datasets. The best value for \\(k\\) is data-dependent and found empirically. A \\(k\\) value too large or too small will impact generalisation.</p> Solution \\(k = 10\\) <p>Where \\(k = 10\\), we can see that our test point would be predicted to be a blue triangle.</p> <p> </p> <p>Note</p> <p>You may have noticed that for \\(k = 10\\), we are using an even number. Ideally, we would choose \\(k\\) to be an odd number to avoid the possibilty of tied votes (i.e. 5 orange dots vs. 5 blue triangles). However, in this instance, we have 4 orange dots and 6 blue triangles, resulting in a blue prediction. </p> <p>When using KNN, it is important to ensure that your choice of \\(k\\) does not allow for a given class (in this case blue triangles) to be the majority of the training data.</p> <p>Theory Question 3</p> <p>In \\(K\\)-Nearest Neighbours, what does \\(K\\) represent?</p> Solution 4 <p>Number of neighbours</p>"},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/1.%20Classification%20Models/0.%20KNN%20%28C%29/#practical-problems","title":"Practical Problems","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/1.%20Classification%20Models/0.%20KNN%20%28C%29/#concluding-notes-and-further-resources","title":"Concluding Notes and Further Resources","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/1.%20Classification%20Models/1.%20DT%20%28C%29/","title":"Decision Trees (Classification)","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/1.%20Classification%20Models/1.%20DT%20%28C%29/#decision-trees-classification","title":"Decision Trees (Classification)\u00b6","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/1.%20Classification%20Models/2.%20RF%20%28C%29/","title":"Random Forests (Classification)","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/1.%20Classification%20Models/2.%20RF%20%28C%29/#random-forests-classification","title":"Random Forests (Classification)\u00b6","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/1.%20Classification%20Models/3.%20LogReg/","title":"Logistic Regression","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/1.%20Classification%20Models/3.%20LogReg/#logistic-regression","title":"Logistic Regression\u00b6","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/1.%20Classification%20Models/4.%20SVM/","title":"Support Vector Machines","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/1.%20Classification%20Models/4.%20SVM/#support-vector-machines","title":"Support Vector Machines\u00b6","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/1.%20Classification%20Models/5.%20Naive%20Bayes/","title":"Naive Bayes","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/1.%20Classification%20Models/5.%20Naive%20Bayes/#naive-bayes","title":"Naive Bayes\u00b6","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/2.%20Regression%20Models/0.%20Linear%20Regression/","title":"Linear Regression","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/2.%20Regression%20Models/1.%20Polynomial%20Regression/","title":"Polynomial Regression","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/2.%20Regression%20Models/2.%20Ridge%20and%20Lasso/","title":"Ridge and Lasso Regression","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/3.%20Evaluation%20Metrics/0.%20Confusion%20Matrix/","title":"Confusion Matrix","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/3.%20Evaluation%20Metrics/1.%20Precision%20Recall%20F1/","title":"Precision, Recall, and F1-Score","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/3.%20Evaluation%20Metrics/2.%20ROC%20and%20AUC/","title":"ROC Curve and AUC","text":""},{"location":"chapters/3.%20Supervised%20Learning/4.%20Model%20S